    hq: "Willoughby, Ohio",
    founded: 1997,
    country: "USA",
    color: "#1f3a5f",
    color2: "#9aa3ad",
    blurb: "Built from Ford's Heavy Truck line, Sterling lived just over a decade — but its trucks still haul freight and parts demand stays strong.",
    models: [
      { model: "A9500", years: "1998–2009", classes: [8], bodyType: "Sleeper", rating: "Up to 80,000 lb GCWR", engines: ["Detroit Series 60", "Cummins ISX", "Caterpillar C15"], hp: "Up to 550 hp", applications: ["Long-haul", "Regional"], tagline: "Sterling's set-back-axle highway tractor.", fact: "The A9500 (A-Line) was Sterling's set-back-axle highway tractor, derived from the aerodynamic Ford AeroMax that Freightliner inherited when it bought Ford's heavy-truck division in 1997. Rather than appearing at the brand's 1998 launch (which fielded the L-Line), the A9500 joined the range in 1999, offered in 113- and 122-inch BBC versions (A9513 and A9522) with engines from Cummins, Caterpillar, Detroit Diesel and Mercedes-Benz. Its flagship SilverStar trim carried an integral 77-inch stand-up sleeper, and the line ran until Sterling was shut down in 2009." },
      { model: "Acterra", years: "1998–2009", classes: [5, 6, 7], bodyType: "Medium-Duty", rating: "Up to 33,000 lb GVWR", engines: ["Mercedes MBE900", "Caterpillar C7"], hp: "Up to 300 hp", applications: ["Delivery", "Dump", "Utility"], tagline: "The medium-duty Sterling workhorse.", fact: "Unveiled in 1999 with production beginning in 2000, the Acterra was Sterling's first true medium-duty line, finally giving a brand that had survived on rebadged Class 8 conventionals a real Class 5 through 8 range. It married a 106-inch-BBC cab derived from the heavy-duty L-Line to a Freightliner Business Class chassis and a standard Mercedes-Benz MBE900 diesel, and stayed in production until Sterling was wound down in 2009." },
      { model: "L-Line (LT7500/9500)", years: "1999–2009", classes: [8], bodyType: "Vocational", rating: "Up to 66,000 lb GVWR", engines: ["Caterpillar C13", "Cummins ISM"], hp: "Up to 475 hp", applications: ["Dump", "Mixer", "Construction"], tagline: "The conventional vocational Sterling.", fact: "The L-Line was a direct continuation of Ford's HN80 Louisville conventional, whose tooling Freightliner acquired in 1997 and put back into production at St. Thomas, Ontario as Sterling's debut model in 1998. CEO James Hebe forecast the L-Line would make up 80 to 85% of Sterling's initial sales, with the urban-distribution LT7500 (101-inch BBC, short sloping hood) at the light end and the vocational LT9500 handling construction, refuse and tanker work until the brand ended in 2009." },
    ],
